question  that was a wonderful answer thank you we  have another question here this one asks  we would like to sequence isolates to  distinguish between outbreaks in our  hospitals from community acquired cases  the idea is to better inform the action  of the ipc teams but our shared ngs core  cannot always return results fast enough  sometimes we receive first data after  more than one month at which point they  are useful for retrospective outbreak  analysis but not for decision making in  the ipc team  how fast can the aeros genetics lab  return isolate sync with excuse me  sequencing data and can you help us  detect outbreak clusters  yes yes this is indeed a problem we also  hear quite frequently  in the community here in europe  on one hand clinical microbiologists try  to find budgets for isolate sequencing  and on the other hand data arrives so  late that the ipc teams can essentially  no longer act on them  other than maybe publishing a  retrospective outbreak analysis  so to to  to give timely input on whether a sample  falls in fact into an outbreak cluster  or is rather community acquired  we we launched the ares iss express  service so rs iss stands for isolate  sequencing and is a sequencing service  we offer  and this uh the expressed version of  this service uh converts isolates into  clear reports they include foreign for  instance information on genotype but  also clustering and we return  samples uh we turn samples around in as  little as five business days  um  for labs that in fact has in-house ngs  capacity and could therefore accelerate  this further  we have also made  aries cloud which is the software that  our own lab uses for end-to-end data  analysis and reporting  and we've made errors cloud commercially  available as a web application as well  great thank you our next question here  asks our hygiene department is  collecting isolates to monitor the  hospital environment for amr pathogens  we would like to try metagenomic  sequencing but are concerned about  sensitivity especially for amr detection  do you have any advice on this  yeah  that's a good question in in theory deep  metagenomic sequencing can detect any  amr marker in a sample  um but especially for let's say  environmental surveillance think think  of example for example of surface swaps  or sink swabs water samples and so on  the bacterial dna in a sample can be so  sparse and the background of unrelated  dna is so high  that shotgun metagenomic sequencing is  starting to struggle  to achieve good sensitivity and  reproducibility  and  metagenomic surveillance essentially is  a sampling problem so the likelihood of  sampling a pathogen or a mr marker when  sequencing such a sample has to be  shifted in favor of a robust detection  and we are doing this by developing  so-called enrichment methods for  instance  rsupa which stands for universal  pathogen msa  combines on one hand ares id which is  our commercially available amplicon  based approach for the identification of  pathogens with on the other hand a  hybridization capture approach so this  approach enriches  for a panel of over 9 000 among markers  which we have selected from the rsdp  so for  any type of environmental surveillance  in the healthcare environment or  otherwise i would certainly recommend to  use an enrichment based method like  rsid or the rs upa i just mentioned  they're both commercially available  although i have to say that the rsupa is  currently only available as uh  for early access testers but we're  certainly happy to help if  if you have a need there  great thank you  another question here asks how can we  watch the previous webinar series  oh uh the previous webinar series are  available on our website you can find  that under www.opgen.com  aries and uh under the aries brand page  you can find resources which include  links to all the ares webinars but i  think it's probably  best if we just share the link with with  you in in  maybe all the attendees in a follow-up  email as this will also be the place  where you can um watch the webinar on  demand and  and also find a future webinars  great thank you  all right another question here do you  have any idea why mycobacterium  abscesses is not included in leading amr  pathogen even though they are highly  resistant to most of the available  antibiotics  i see so  non-tuberculosis  microbacteria like mycobacterium  abscesses  they certainly  increase more contribute more and more  to healthcare-associated infections but  i think the reason it wasn't included in  the study that the question is referring  to  is probably due to the fact that the  study focused on respiratory tract  infections and as far as i know does uh  mycobacterium abscesses um  rarely contribute to um  to respiratory tract infections um  you it is much more common in soft  tissue infections skin infections and so  on and i believe that might be the  reason why it was not included  fantastic well we are going to go ahead  and wrap up with that final question  there i want to thank you again dr  materna for your time today and your  important research we would also like to  thank labyrinths and our sponsor opjen  for underwriting today's educational  webcast before we go i'd like to thank  the audience for joining us today and  for their interesting questions  questions we do not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can also be viewed on  demand laborats will alert you via email  when it's available for replay we  encourage you to share that email with  your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event until next time take  care everyone goodbye  you

